 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Victoria's labour market has softened for women over the past year, though employment is still slightly higher than a year ago. Latest figures are for September 2026 (ABS Labour Force, seasonally adjusted):\n\n- **Unemployment rate:** 5.1%, up from about 4.0% in late 2025 and a low of 3.9% in April 2025. It has been above 5% since June 2026. Women's unemployment is now slightly above the Victorian total of 5.1%. At the start of 2025 it was 4.8%, compared with 4.7% for all Victorians.\n- **Employed:** about 1,843,000 women, roughly 5,000 more than in September 2025. Numbers dipped in early 2026 and have recovered since August.\n- **Participation rate:** 63.6%, down from 63.9% a year earlier. Women's participation is lower than the overall Victorian rate of 67.4%.\n- **Employment-to-population ratio:** 60.4%, compared with 61.3% in September 2025. This is the clearest sign of weakening, since it shows a smaller share of working-age women with jobs.\n\nOverall, women are still finding work in numbers, but unemployment has risen since late 2025 and fewer women are in the labour force. Monthly estimates are noisy, so the trend over several months matters more than any single reading.",
